# Synthetic probe corpus for the private-content firewall (arqtiqa/arqtos#361).
#
# WHAT THIS IS. One synthetic probe per active rule in
# `.github/scripts/private-content-denylist.txt`, plus benign lookalikes that
# must NOT fire. `.github/actions/firewall-falsifier` asserts, on every PR:
#
#   * every active rule fires on at least one [must-match] probe
#     -> a rule edited into a compiling-but-non-matching form, or added with
#        nothing behind it, turns the build RED
#   * every [must-match] probe fires at least one rule
#     -> a DELETED rule turns the build RED, via its orphaned probe. Counting
#        violations cannot see this: delete a rule and both sides drop by one.
#   * no [must-not-match] lookalike fires anything
#     -> a rule widened until it matches ordinary content turns the build RED
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. Before this, this repository's denylist rested on a one-time
# manual dry-run performed when the gate was enabled. Every way a denylist
# decays leaves the gate GREEN, and a green firewall over a dead denylist is
# worse than no firewall, because it is trusted.
#
# ⚠️ EVERY STRING BELOW IS SYNTHETIC. A probe is shaped LIKE what a rule
# catches and is never an instance of it: no real credential, no real identity
# value, no real host. This repository is PUBLIC and this file is committed.
#
# ⚠️ THIS FILE FIRES THE REAL GATE ON ITSELF, BY CONSTRUCTION, AND THAT IS
# HANDLED IN THE OPEN. It is a file full of denylist-shaped strings, so the
# firewall matches it. It is exempted by one reviewed `.firewallignore` entry
# per rule, each naming this exact path and that one rule, each with its
# mandatory reason. It is deliberately NOT excluded from the scan inside the
# tooling: a silent exclusion is the defect class arqtiqa/arqtos#343 was filed
# for, where a file was dropped from the scan before `.firewallignore` could
# even record a decision about it. Those entries are fail-closed -- edit or
# delete a rule and its entry goes stale, which is exit 2, not a silent no-op.
#
# ⚠️ KEEP THIS FILE IN STEP WITH THE DENYLIST, IN THE SAME COMMIT. Adding a
# rule without a probe is red. Removing a rule without removing its probe is
# red. That coupling is the mechanism, not an inconvenience.
#
# A probe is the WHOLE LINE; there are no fields. A probe therefore cannot
# begin with `#` or `[`, and each one is described by the comment above it.

[must-match]

# private-network address range
192.168.0.0

# GitHub personal access token (classic prefix)
ghp_ZZZSYNTHETICFIREWALLPROBE00000000000

# GitHub OAuth token
gho_ZZZSYNTHETICFIREWALLPROBE00000000000

# GitHub fine-grained personal access token
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

# Linear API key
# ⚠️ PREFIXED WITH `ZZZSYNTHETIC`, UNLIKE ITS NEIGHBOURS, AND THE PREFIX IS
# LOAD-BEARING. GitHub's own PUSH PROTECTION scans this file like any other and
# refused the first push of this corpus, flagging this one line. Its detectors
# for the GitHub and AWS shapes above carry a checksum that an obviously-fake
# body fails, so those pass; the Linear shape is prefix-plus-length alone, so a
# synthetic body satisfies it exactly.
#
# ⚠️ THE FIX IS THE PREFIX, NEVER THE "ALLOW THIS SECRET" LINK. Clicking that
# link whitelists the string estate-wide and teaches a reviewer that push
# protection is something you click past -- which is the same "a permanently
# red gate gets disabled rather than obeyed" failure the exemptions mechanism
# exists to avoid, on a control nobody here owns.
#
# The rule this probe exercises has no leading word boundary, so an embedded
# occurrence is a genuine match for it; the third-party detector wants the
# prefix to start a token, and here it does not. If the rule is ever narrowed
# with a `\b`, this probe stops firing and the build goes red -- which is
# correct: a narrowed rule must be re-proven, not assumed.
ZZZSYNTHETIClin_api_ZZZSYNTHETICFIREWALLPROBE000000000000000

# 1Password secret reference
op://zzz-synthetic-vault/zzz-synthetic-item

# GitHub user-to-server token
ghu_ZZZSYNTHETICFIREWALLPROBE00000000000

# GitHub server-to-server token
ghs_ZZZSYNTHETICFIREWALLPROBE00000000000

# GitHub refresh token
ghr_ZZZSYNTHETICFIREWALLPROBE00000000000

# AWS access key id
AKIAZZZZSYNTHETICPRB

# PEM private-key block header
# ⚠️ Begins with hyphens. `grep` parses a leading-hyphen pattern as an OPTION
# unless `-e` is used -- the live defect arqtos-cli#1069 filed, where the
# highest-severity rule in the list was silently never applied in CI while the
# check stayed green. This probe is the standing proof that it is applied.
-----BEGIN ZZZ SYNTHETIC PROBE PRIVATE KEY-----

[must-not-match]

# an ordinary sentence, no credential shape anywhere in it
the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog

# a dotted quad that stops one octet short of the private-range rule
192.168.0

# just OUTSIDE the private 172.16-172.31 block -- pins the range boundary, so
# widening the rule to swallow the whole 172/8 space turns the build red
172.15.10.1

# GitHub token prefix with a body far too short to be a token
ghp_ZZZSYNTHETICTOOSHORT

# AWS key prefix with a body too short to be a key id
AKIAZZZSHORT

# a secret-reference scheme with a vault but no item -- an incomplete
# reference is not a reference
op://zzz-synthetic-vault

# a PEM block header for PUBLIC key material, which is not a secret
-----BEGIN ZZZ SYNTHETIC PROBE PUBLIC KEY-----
